#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168050 :tutorial/content "Recall that the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_A(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x \\in A$ and $0$ if $x \\notin A.$ \n![](images/c1/c12d2f59eb7eb9730fc7576bb426cff90a3cff4ac8577c3f212db8101197300b.svg)\n\nIn this lesson, we introduce a more general notation that allows us to write indicator functions for any condition, not just set membership.\n\nThe **Iverson bracket notation** $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ takes a predicate $P$ and returns\n![](images/4d/4dbe29e205fecf97bd7d4c0bfbf810c7336a6a50198fa35319e7ae8b0ac505c0.svg)\n\nFor example:\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[3 > 2] = 1$ because $3 > 2$ is true.\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[5 \\leq 4] = 0$ because $5 \\leq 4$ is false.\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[7 \\in \\{2, 5, 7\\}] = 1$ because $7$ is an element of $\\{2, 5, 7\\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "83dcb8a1-56ef-59d0-addc-dfaa18628695" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14816 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168051 :tutorial/content "Indicator functions are useful for writing expressions that behave differently depending on whether a condition is met.\n\n**Sums with indicator functions.** When we multiply a term by an indicator function inside a sum, the indicator acts as a filter. For example, given values $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ the sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0] \\cdot x_i\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values. If $x_i > 0,$ the indicator equals $1$ and the term contributes $x_i.$ If $x_i \\leq 0,$ the indicator equals $0$ and the term contributes nothing.\n\nConsider the values $x_1 = 5, x_2 = -3, x_3 = 2.$ Using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =1[5>0]\\cdot 5+1[-3>0]\\cdot (-3)+1[2>0]\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 5+0\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5+0+2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0]$ counts the positive values. For this data, the count is $1 + 0 + 1 = 2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dc59a9ec-2473-5942-9ab7-f81210d7426c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14817 :tutorial/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions: Sums"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168059 :tutorial/content "The set-based notation from the previous lesson is a special case of Iverson bracket notation. Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_A(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\in A].\n$$\n\nBoth expressions equal $1$ when $x \\in A$ and $0$ when $x \\notin A.$ \n\nThe Iverson bracket is more flexible because it works with any predicate, such as inequalities. For example:\n![](images/cd/cd01819adb510c2b439f44089b1bd3cbd1f49e671779039fdf2f6be62a39810f.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "9a719d20-4e48-50a9-9e39-db7296bd47d4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14942 :tutorial/title "Set Notation and Bracket Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168060 :tutorial/content "Indicator functions can also express piecewise functions compactly. Consider the absolute value function:\n![](images/33/336dc8080661db77e7ac701204aa6a32075bc7f4bd06625f4e8467e87a5c075c.svg)\n\nWe can write this as a single expression using indicator functions:\n\n$$\n|x| = \\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 0] \\cdot x + \\mathbf{1}[x < 0] \\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nSince the conditions $x \\geq 0$ and $x < 0$ are mutually exclusive, exactly one indicator equals $1$ and the other equals $0.$ This \"switch\" mechanism selects the appropriate term automatically.\n\nLet's verify this expression for a negative number, say $x = -4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|-4| & =1[-4\\ge 0]\\cdot (-4)+1[-4<0]\\cdot (-(-4)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50ebf85-232e-5ea7-aed0-188bbb2467bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14963 :tutorial/title "Using Indicator Functions in Expressions: Piecewise Functions"}]}
